✅ OPTION A — Multi-Stage Dockerfile (Best for Spring Boot Java Apps)

Goal → Build JAR inside Docker → Produce small runtime image
Compatible with ARM (M1/M2) ✔

##Dockerfile

dockerfile
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# STAGE 1: BUILD STAGE
# -----------------------------------------------------------
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jdk AS build

WORKDIR /app

COPY mvnw .
COPY .mvn .mvn
COPY pom.xml .
RUN ./mvnw dependency:resolve

COPY src src
RUN ./mvnw clean package -DskipTests

# -----------------------------------------------------------
# STAGE 2: RUNTIME STAGE
# -----------------------------------------------------------
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre

ARG JAR_FILE=/app/target/*.jar
COPY --from=build ${JAR_FILE} app.jar

EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]


##.dockerignore


target/
.git
.gitignore
.idea/
*.iml


##Build & Run


docker build -t test-poc:multi .
docker run -p 8080:8080 test-poc:multi


To run that container in the background (detached mode), add the -d flag. Example:

sudo docker run -d -p 8080:8080 test-poc:multi

Notes and useful options:
- -d runs detached (background).
- --name mycontainer gives a friendly name:  
  sudo docker run -d --name test-poc -p 8080:8080 test-poc:multi
- -e KEY=VALUE sets environment variables if needed.
- -v host_path:container_path mounts volumes (persistent data).
- --restart unless-stopped restarts container after reboots/crashes:  
  sudo docker run -d --restart unless-stopped --name test-poc -p 8080:8080 test-poc:multi
- To see logs: sudo docker logs -f test-poc
- To list running containers: sudo docker ps
- To stop: sudo docker stop test-poc; remove: sudo docker rm test-poc

If the container exposes additional ports or requires network/config 

